"""Builds pilots/L04/job.json — Operation Northwoods (long-form, ~7-8 min).
Primary source: JCS memo 13 March 1962 (pages in assets/, OCR). Context: ABC News (Bamford, 2001), Wikipedia 'Operation Northwoods' (ARRB release 18 Nov 1997; Lansdale memo of 16 March 1962)."""
import json, pathlib, re
CH = [
 ("The memo", """[serious] In March 1962, the most senior officers in the United States military approved a plan. It proposed staging attacks on Americans. Blowing up an American ship. Faking the shoot-down of a plane full of students. A fake terror campaign in Miami, and even in Washington. And then blaming all of it on Cuba. It was never carried out. But it was written down, approved by the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and handed to the Secretary of Defense. For thirty-five years, almost nobody knew it existed. This is the complete case file on Operation Northwoods. Let's open it."""),
 ("Why Cuba", """To understand Northwoods, you have to understand how the American government felt about Cuba in 1962. Three years earlier, Fidel Castro had taken power, ninety miles from Florida. In April 1961, the C.I.A.'s Bay of Pigs invasion had collapsed in days. It was a humiliation for the new president, John F. Kennedy. So the administration launched a secret program to bring Castro down from the inside. It was called Operation Mongoose, and its chief of operations asked the military for something very specific. He wanted brief but precise descriptions of pretexts, reasons, that would justify an American invasion. The Joint Chiefs had already said what they thought. If a revolt inside Cuba could not be achieved, the memo records them saying, the United States would need to develop a provocation. Northwoods was their answer."""),
 ("The pretexts", """The document is titled, Justification for U.S. Military Intervention in Cuba. It is stamped top secret. The plan's starting point was simple. It would be desirable, the authors wrote, to use legitimate provocation as the basis for military action. And if there wasn't one, they would make one. The first ideas centered on the American base at Guantanamo Bay. Start rumors. Use clandestine radio. Land friendly Cubans, dressed in Cuban uniforms, to stage an attack on the base. Start riots near the main gate. Blow up ammunition inside the base, and start fires. Burn aircraft. Lob mortar shells into the base from outside. Sink a ship near the harbor entrance, and then, in the memo's own words, conduct funerals for mock victims. Then came what the authors called a Remember the Maine incident, named after the American warship whose explosion helped start the Spanish-American War. We could blow up a U.S. ship in Guantanamo Bay, the memo says, and blame Cuba. And they were clear about why. Casualty lists in U.S. newspapers, they wrote, would cause a helpful wave of national indignation."""),
 ("Terror at home", """The plan didn't stop at Cuba's shores. We could develop a Communist Cuban terror campaign, the document says, in the Miami area, in other Florida cities, and even in Washington. It suggests sinking a boatload of Cubans on their way to Florida, real or simulated. It suggests attempts on the lives of Cuban refugees in the United States, even to the extent of wounding, in instances to be widely publicized. Exploding a few plastic bombs in carefully chosen spots, it continues, along with arrests of Cuban agents and the release of prepared documents, would help project the idea of an irresponsible government. Read that again. A plan written by America's own military, proposing a terror campaign in American cities, with plastic bombs, and attacks on refugees who had fled to the United States for safety. All so that the blame would land somewhere else. And it went further still. The memo suggests faking a Cuban attack on a neighboring country. Aircraft disguised as Cuban planes could make night raids over the Dominican Republic, it says, and Soviet-made incendiary devices could be left behind to be found. Fake Cuban messages to the local Communist underground, and fake Cuban arms shipments discovered on the beach, would complete the picture. Hijacking attempts against civilian planes and boats, it adds, should appear to continue as harassing measures, condoned by the government of Cuba."""),
 ("The airliner", """Then there were the aircraft. One proposal was to paint an American F-86 fighter to look like a Cuban MiG, so that airline passengers would be convinced they had seen one, especially if their own pilot announced it. But the most elaborate plan was this. Create an incident, the memo says, that would demonstrate convincingly that a Cuban aircraft had shot down a chartered civilian airliner flying from the United States. The passengers could be a group of students off on a holiday. Here's how it would work. A plane at Eglin Air Force Base in Florida would be painted as an exact duplicate of a real civilian aircraft. The passengers, boarded under carefully prepared aliases, would get on the duplicate. South of Florida, the two planes would meet. The duplicate carrying the passengers would drop to minimum altitude and land at an auxiliary field at Eglin, where the passengers would be taken off. Meanwhile, the real aircraft, converted into an empty drone, would continue on the filed flight plan. Over Cuba, it would send a Mayday call, saying it was under attack by Cuban MiGs. And then, the memo says, the transmission would be interrupted by the destruction of the aircraft, triggered by radio signal. A second plan described a fake shoot-down of an American fighter, with a pilot who would vanish under an alias and debris scattered on the sea for searchers to find."""),
 ("No", """On March 13th, 1962, General Lyman Lemnitzer, Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, presented the plan to Defense Secretary Robert McNamara. The document had the approval of all the Joint Chiefs. It even included instructions that it should not be shared with American commanders in the field, with NATO, or with the United Nations. Three days later, at a meeting at the White House, President Kennedy made his position clear. A record of that meeting has him saying that they were not discussing the use of military force. The plan went nowhere. Within months, Lemnitzer was out as Chairman of the Joint Chiefs. By January 1963, he was in Europe, as NATO's Supreme Allied Commander in Europe. None of the attacks in Northwoods ever happened."""),
 ("Thirty-five years", """For decades, the documents stayed buried. Then, in 1991, Oliver Stone's film JFK reignited public suspicion about the Kennedy assassination. In response, Congress passed a law in 1992 forcing the release of government records connected to the case. A review board began working through thousands of pages. On November 18th, 1997, it released a batch of military records from the early 1960s. Among them were the Northwoods documents. Few people noticed at first. It took a 2001 book by the journalist James Bamford to bring the plan to wide attention."""),
 ("What the file tells us", """So what does Operation Northwoods really tell us? Not that the American government carried out attacks on its own people. It didn't. A president said no, and the plan died. But it does tell us something uncomfortable. That in 1962, the highest military officers in the country were willing to put in writing, and approve, a plan to deceive the American public into supporting a war, using fake attacks, fake victims, and real bombs. It tells us that the line was held by civilian leaders, not by the system itself. And it tells us how long a secret like this can stay hidden. This memo was written, approved, rejected, and then filed away. If not for a movie and a new law, we might never have seen it. Send this to someone who thinks every conspiracy theory is crazy. This is Dossier Decoded. Case file: Operation Northwoods. Rejected. Declassified."""),
]
job = {"slug": "L04-northwoods", "pillar": "Turned out true", "format": "long-form",
 "script": " ".join(t for _, t in CH), "chapters": [{"title": t, "text": x} for t, x in CH],
 "pdf": None, "commons": [],
 "sfx": [{"slug": "drone", "text": "dark low ambient drone, tense documentary underscore, slow pulsing sub bass, no melody, seamless", "seconds": 22},
  {"slug": "stamp", "text": "a heavy rubber stamp slamming down on paper on a wooden desk, single hit, close up", "seconds": 1.2},
  {"slug": "paper", "text": "a single sheet of paper being slid across a desk and flipped, close up", "seconds": 1.2},
  {"slug": "hit", "text": "deep cinematic impact hit, single boom, short tail", "seconds": 1.5},
  {"slug": "typewriter", "text": "fast mechanical typewriter keys clacking, short burst", "seconds": 2},
  {"slug": "radio", "text": "1960s aircraft radio distress call static, crackle and cut off", "seconds": 3}]}
p = pathlib.Path(__file__).parent
(p / "job.json").write_text(json.dumps(job, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))
w = len(re.sub(r"\[.*?\]", "", job["script"]).split())
print("words", w, "est min", round(w / 2.85 / 60, 1))
